{"schema_version": "demo-1.0", "recorder_version": "demo-1.0", "event_id": "event_0055", "episode_id": "episode_sample_0001", "session_id": "session_sample_0001", "global_sequence": 55, "kind": "DOCUMENT_FINAL_TEXT", "evidence_grade": "OBSERVED", "observed_at": "2026-05-14T09:06:25.000Z", "chars": 751, "text": "Photosynthesis notes\n\nPlants are green and they make food from sunlight. I think chlorophyll is involved but I'm not sure how. Need to understand the actual steps and where the oxygen comes from.\n\nPhotosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy: 6CO2 + 6H2O + light -> C6H12O6 + 6O2. The oxygen released comes from splitting water (photolysis), not from CO2. Light-dependent reactions in the thylakoid make ATP and NADPH and release O2; the Calvin cycle in the stroma uses that ATP and NADPH to fix CO2 into glucose, and does not need light directly. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green, which is why leaves look green. My summary in my own words: sunlight powers a two-stage process that turns air and water into food.", "previous_hash": "03d49a265577aad1a071188d7c3888f9e81f45554e34137f8e634961fe2e8ec7", "event_hash": "e05a5e1c4b13d0ba9cda7a8295d7a53c639dba7278d8f3eb5ef988a51363a2f0"}
